{
  "Bug_Title": "Eq: equals method overrides equals in superclass and may not be symmetric (EQ_OVERRIDING_EQUALS_NOT_SYMMETRIC)\uf0c1",
  "Bug_Details": " This class defines an equals method that overrides an equals method in a superclass. Both equals methods\nuse instanceof in the determination of whether two objects are equal. This is fraught with peril,\nsince it is important that the equals method is symmetrical (in other words, a.equals(b) == b.equals(a)).\nIf B is a subtype of A, and A's equals method checks that the argument is an instanceof A, and B's equals method\nchecks that the argument is an instanceof B, it is quite likely that the equivalence relation defined by these\nmethods is not symmetric.\n instanceof a.equals(b) == b.equals(a)",
  "Bug_Explanation": "This class 'SubClass' overrides the equals method in the superclass 'SuperClass'. The equals method in the superclass checks if the argument is an instance of 'SuperClass' and returns true. The equals method in the subclass, 'SubClass', is symmetric, but it also checks if the argument is an instance of 'SubClass'. If 'SuperClass' and 'SubClass' have a subtype relationship and we are comparing objects of those subtypes, the symmetry of the equals method is violated. The equals method in 'SuperClass' returns true when 'SubClass' instance is passed as argument and vice versa. This is because 'SubClass' instance is also a 'SuperClass' instance.",
  "Buggy_Line": [
    16
  ],
  "Seed_Compilation_Status": "Successful",
  "Trigger_Explanation": "This test case triggers the bug because it compares the output of equals method in SuperClass with SubClass and vice versa. The bug is that the equals method in SubClass only checks if the argument is an instance of SubClass, while the equals method in SuperClass checks if the argument is an instance of SuperClass. This makes the equals method asymmetric, causing the test to fail.",
